r_authorizing_one) ... ok\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:29: trailing whitespace.\n+test_one_sided_ambiguity_clears_all_selected_fields_in_helper_and_build_evidence (tests.test_regression_change_loop.NativeBuildAmbiguityRegressionTests.test_one_sided_ambiguity_clears_all_selected_fields_in_helper_and_build_evidence) ... ok\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:30: trailing whitespace.\n+test_recovery_revokes_assignment_before_cleanup_failure (tests.test_regression_change_loop.RecoveryCleanupRegressionTests.test_recovery_revokes_assignment_before_cleanup_failure) ... ok\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:31: trailing whitespace.\n+test_successful_recovery_closes_both_resources_and_requires_fresh_connection (tests.test_regression_change_loop.RecoveryCleanupRegressionTests.test_successful_recovery_closes_both_resources_and_requires_fresh_connection) ... ok\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:32: trailing whitespace.\n+test_adapter_reset_connect_does_not_reintroduce_ambient_routing_but_open_does (tests.test_regression_change_loop.ResetConnectRoutingRegressionTests.test_adapter_reset_connect_does_not_reintroduce_ambient_routing_but_open_does) ... ok\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:33: trailing whitespace.\n+test_reset_connect_ignores_ambient_uid_and_target_but_normal_resolution_keeps_compatibility (tests.test_regression_change_loop.ResetConnectRoutingRegressionTests.test_reset_connect_ignores_ambient_uid_and_target_but_normal_resolution_keeps_compatibility) ... ok\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:34: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:35: trailing whitespace.\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:36: trailing whitespace.\n+Ran 11 tests in 0.098s\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:37: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/archive/setup-relocking-20260723/state/test_report.md:38: trailing whitespace.\n+OK\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:1: trailing whitespace.\n+# Setup plan relocking bug\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:2: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:3: trailing whitespace.\n+Fix only the setup-authorization bookkeeping defect where closing stale setup\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:4: trailing whitespace.\n+allowance P1 can invalidate replacement PlanEngine plan P2 for the same board.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:5: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:6: trailing whitespace.\n+Required behavior:\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:7: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:8: trailing whitespace.\n+1. SetupWorkflow allowance-close callbacks carry `board_id`, `allowance_id`,\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:9: trailing whitespace.\n+   and `reason`.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:10: trailing whitespace.\n+2. `PlanEngine.complete_paired_plan()` accepts optional `expected_plan_id` and\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:11: trailing whitespace.\n+   only consumes permission / invalidates when the active plan matches it.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:12: trailing whitespace.\n+3. Recheck the matching active plan after permission cleanup so a concurrently\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:13: trailing whitespace.\n+   installed replacement is not invalidated.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:14: trailing whitespace.\n+4. Server setup allowance closure passes the closing allowance ID.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:15: trailing whitespace.\n+5. Setup tool wrappers pass the allowance/plan ID wherever already known.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:16: trailing whitespace.\n+6. Replacing incomplete P1 with P2 must leave P2 active and its paired\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:17: trailing whitespace.\n+   `board_fix_setup` callable; closing matching P1 must still relock normally.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:18: trailing whitespace.\n+7. Cover the full external-UART continuation route and replacement-during-\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:19: trailing whitespace.\n+   completion race with automated tests.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:20: trailing whitespace.\n+8. Scope the adjacent loader allowance and continuation cleanup by the same\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:21: trailing whitespace.\n+   expected allowance identity. Reorder binding if necessary so synchronous P1\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:22: trailing whitespace.\n+   retirement clears P1 state before P2 becomes the loader's current allowance.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:23: trailing whitespace.\n+   A late P1 callback/wrapper must not clear P2's loader allowance, accepted\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:24: trailing whitespace.\n+   selections, or continuation facts.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:25: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:26: trailing whitespace.\n+Follow `../.codex/design_charter.md`: use identity-based correctness rather\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:27: trailing whitespace.\n+than broad guards, preserve one-time permission boundaries, keep the change\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:28: trailing whitespace.\n+general and simple, and do not introduce board-, port-, or OS-specific logic.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:29: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:30: trailing whitespace.\n+The broader limitation around multiple sequential repair passes is explicitly\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:31: trailing whitespace.\n+out of scope.\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:32: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:33: trailing whitespace.\n+Preserve all unrelated pre-existing working-tree changes, including the earlier\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:34: trailing whitespace.\n+UTF-8 probe-inventory fix. Do not edit or restore deleted `testing_folder`\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:35: trailing whitespace.\n+content; a fresh hardware-only `testing_folder` will be created after the\r\n.change-loop/changes.md:36: trailing whitespace.\n+software change loop is green.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:1: trailing whitespace.\n+# MCP Firmware Server — Design Charter & Agent Operating Goals\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:2: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:3: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:4: trailing whitespace.\n+## Mission\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:5: trailing whitespace.\n+Use this charter in addition to the main prompt being given. Wait/Listen/Review for the main prompt input before doing anything. I will supply it to you or have already supplied it to you. Do not return an answer unless the main prompt is actually blocked by something unpassable, or the task in the main prompt has been fully executed to completion. When executing the main prompt, for ANY changes made to the actual MCP server, build and maintain an MCP server that lets an AI agent do real firmware\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:6: trailing whitespace.\n+engineering — discover and configure targets, build, flash, debug, inspect,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:7: trailing whitespace.\n+and iterate — across **arbitrary hardware, toolchains, and host environments**,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:8: trailing whitespace.\n+without a human having to hand-hold the setup for each new part.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:9: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:10: trailing whitespace.\n+This charter is the goal to keep in mind for any active changes to the existing MCP server.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:11: trailing whitespace.\n+You should not edit parts that are not broken / did not ask to be edited, even if you think\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:12: trailing whitespace.\n+parts of the server you have read did not fully line up to this charter - those parts work, and I \r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:13: trailing whitespace.\n+don't want to break them.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:14: trailing whitespace.\n+This document is the standard every change is measured against. When a design\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:15: trailing whitespace.\n+decision is unclear, resolve it toward these goals using the tie-breakers in\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:16: trailing whitespace.\n+*When principles collide*, and **state the assumption you made** (in the tool's\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:17: trailing whitespace.\n+output, its docstring, or a code comment — wherever the next reader will see it).\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:18: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:19: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:20: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:21: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:22: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:23: trailing whitespace.\n+## The four core properties\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:24: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:25: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:26: trailing whitespace.\n+### 1. Correctness — behave the way the idea intends\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:27: trailing whitespace.\n+The server does what a competent firmware engineer would expect, not an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:28: trailing whitespace.\n+approximation of it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:29: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:30: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:31: trailing whitespace.\n+- A `flash` tool actually writes **and verifies**; a `read_register` returns the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:32: trailing whitespace.\n+  true value; a `reset` leaves the device in a genuinely known post-reset state.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:33: trailing whitespace.\n+- **No silent failure and no fabrication.** If a flash fails verification, the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:34: trailing whitespace.\n+  tool reports failure — it never returns success. If the server cannot\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:35: trailing whitespace.\n+  determine something (e.g. it can't read a chip ID), it says so rather than\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:36: trailing whitespace.\n+  guessing a plausible-looking answer.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:37: trailing whitespace.\n+- Anything that deviates from intended behavior — wrong logic, a functionality\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:38: trailing whitespace.\n+  gap, a bug, a swallowed error — gets replaced with an implementation that\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:39: trailing whitespace.\n+  matches the intent.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:40: trailing whitespace.\n+- If the spec is ambiguous, resolve to the behavior the design most clearly\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:41: trailing whitespace.\n+  implies (e.g. \"reset\" almost always means \"get the part into a clean known\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:42: trailing whitespace.\n+  state,\" so do the reset that achieves that) and record the assumption.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:43: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:44: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:45: trailing whitespace.\n+### 2. Simplicity — Occam's razor\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:46: trailing whitespace.\n+The simplest form that is still highly effective.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:47: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:48: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:49: trailing whitespace.\n+- Each tool does **one** job well. No 40-knob config where 3 knobs cover the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:50: trailing whitespace.\n+  real cases.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:51: trailing whitespace.\n+- No speculative abstraction, no gold-plating, no plugin framework built for a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:52: trailing whitespace.\n+  single plugin, no machinery that isn't earning its keep.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:53: trailing whitespace.\n+- Prefer flat, obvious control flow. If wrapping an existing, battle-tested tool\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:54: trailing whitespace.\n+  (OpenOCD, probe-rs, esptool, avrdude, etc.) does the job, wrap it — don't\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:55: trailing whitespace.\n+  reimplement a protocol.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:56: trailing whitespace.\n+- Litmus test: a new engineer should be able to understand a tool's\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:57: trailing whitespace.\n+  implementation in one sitting.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:58: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:59: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:60: trailing whitespace.\n+### 3. Generalizability — works for setups it has never seen\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:61: trailing whitespace.\n+The code must work for any reasonable hardware, OS, and software environment —\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:62: trailing whitespace.\n+not just the one it was tested on.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:63: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:64: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:65: trailing whitespace.\n+- **No environment-specific constants.** No hardcoded `/dev/ttyUSB0` or `COM3`,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:66: trailing whitespace.\n+  no \"Linux-only\" assumptions, no baked-in core/GPU counts, no fixed toolchain\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:67: trailing whitespace.\n+  Paths, no fixed build environments, no specific toolchains EVER. This should work for EVERY board, EVERY build system, EVERY repo, EVERY environment, and EVERY part.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:68: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Detect, parameterize, or degrade gracefully — in that order.** Discover\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:69: trailing whitespace.\n+  serial ports, debug probes, chip IDs, and installed toolchains at runtime.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:70: trailing whitespace.\n+  Parameterize what varies. If auto-detection fails, expose a tool for the agent\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:71: trailing whitespace.\n+  to supply the missing piece — and persist it so the gap closes permanently.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:72: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Autonomous onboarding.** The setup path should take an unknown board and\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:73: trailing whitespace.\n+  bring it to the same known-good state a supported board reaches, on its own.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:74: trailing whitespace.\n+  Onboarding a new MCU must never require editing source.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:75: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:76: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:77: trailing whitespace.\n+### 4. Neatness — organized for agent and human alike\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:78: trailing whitespace.\n+No sloppiness, no tangled web.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:79: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:80: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:81: trailing whitespace.\n+- Consistent naming: tools read as `verb_noun`; shared parameters keep the same\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:82: trailing whitespace.\n+  name and units across every tool (`port`, `target`, `elf_path`, `baud`).\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:83: trailing whitespace.\n+- One home per responsibility — detection logic lives in one place, not smeared\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:84: trailing whitespace.\n+  across five tools.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:85: trailing whitespace.\n+- No dead code, no commented-out experiments, no confusing control flow. Tool\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:86: trailing whitespace.\n+  interfaces are predictable enough that the shape of the next tool is guessable\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:87: trailing whitespace.\n+  from the last.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:88: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:89: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:90: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:91: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:92: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:93: trailing whitespace.\n+## 5. Usability — the server must teach its user how to use it\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:94: trailing whitespace.\n+Simplicity includes being intuitive to operate, for both an agent and a human.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:95: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:96: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:97: trailing whitespace.\n+- Every tool description states: **what it does, when to reach for it, its\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:98: trailing whitespace.\n+  parameters (with units and an example), what it returns, and its common\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:99: trailing whitespace.\n+  failure modes with the recovery step.**\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:100: trailing whitespace.\n+- Ship a top-level \"start here\" resource the agent can read first: the normal\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:101: trailing whitespace.\n+  workflow (`detect → configure → build → flash → verify → debug`) and how the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:102: trailing whitespace.\n+  tools chain together.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:103: trailing whitespace.\n+- The agent should never have to guess a contract or discover behavior by\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:104: trailing whitespace.\n+  trial. If it needs outside knowledge to use a tool correctly, the tool's docs\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:105: trailing whitespace.\n+  are incomplete.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:106: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:107: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:108: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:109: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:110: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:111: trailing whitespace.\n+## 6. Dynamism — solve the novel case with the tools, not a code change\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:112: trailing whitespace.\n+This is the differentiator. When the agent meets a board, chip, or toolchain\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:113: trailing whitespace.\n+the server has never seen, it should be able to succeed using the tools that\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:114: trailing whitespace.\n+already exist.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:115: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:116: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:117: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Introspection first:** tools to enumerate what's connected and identify what\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:118: trailing whitespace.\n+  it is (probe list, port scan, chip-ID read, toolchain discovery).\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:119: trailing whitespace.\n+- **A generic path** to talk to a target over its standard interface\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:120: trailing whitespace.\n+  (SWD/JTAG/UART/DFU) even when no named profile for that exact part exists yet.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:121: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Learn and persist:** a way for the agent to register what it figured out so\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:122: trailing whitespace.\n+  the previously-unknown part is *known* on the next run.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:123: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:124: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:125: trailing whitespace.\n+Design tools to be composable and general enough that \"I've never seen this\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:126: trailing whitespace.\n+part\" is handled by the agent orchestrating existing tools — **not** by someone\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:127: trailing whitespace.\n+adding another branch to a `switch` statement.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:128: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:129: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:130: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:131: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:132: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:133: trailing whitespace.\n+## 7. Operating assumptions — design for the normal case\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:134: trailing whitespace.\n+Build for ordinary firmware work under normal conditions, not for exotic edge\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:135: trailing whitespace.\n+cases that won't happen.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:136: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:137: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:138: trailing whitespace.\n+- Assume a **compliant, non-malicious agent** and a **cooperative user** doing\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:139: trailing whitespace.\n+  regular firmware tasks with regular firmware parts. *Compliant does not mean\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:140: trailing whitespace.\n+  infallible.* Assume the agent is only OK at its job: it follows tool contracts\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:141: trailing whitespace.\n+  in good faith but will make honest mistakes — misread returned state, act on a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:142: trailing whitespace.\n+  stale or unverified assumption, select the wrong target, or thrash in a retry\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:143: trailing whitespace.\n+  loop. The threat model excludes the **hostile** actor; it does **not** exclude\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:144: trailing whitespace.\n+  the **fallible** one. Guarding against the agent's own mistakes is in scope;\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:145: trailing whitespace.\n+  hardening against an attacker is not.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:146: trailing whitespace.\n+- **The agent and the user are inside the trust boundary — but the boundary is\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:147: trailing whitespace.\n+  about hostility, not competence.** Anything they hand the server — a pack file,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:148: trailing whitespace.\n+  a path, an ELF, a device selection, a build command — is *trusted input* in the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:149: trailing whitespace.\n+  sense that it is not an attack. It is **not** assumed to be *correct*. Validate\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:150: trailing whitespace.\n+  it for **correctness** (is it the right, real thing; does it match verified\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:151: trailing whitespace.\n+  state; will it make the tool behave correctly?), never for **hostility**. The\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:152: trailing whitespace.\n+  server does not treat its operator as an attacker, but it does treat the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:153: trailing whitespace.\n+  operator as capable of error — it catches the mistake rather than faithfully\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:154: trailing whitespace.\n+  executing it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:155: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Handle the failure modes that actually occur** every day: no device\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:156: trailing whitespace.\n+  connected, wrong port, insufficient permission to open the port, build errors,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:157: trailing whitespace.\n+  flash/verify mismatch, a probe that dropped off the bus, a brownout mid-flash.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:158: trailing whitespace.\n+  **Also handle the mistakes a mediocre agent actually makes:** flashing an ELF\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:159: trailing whitespace.\n+  whose target doesn't match the detected chip, issuing a destructive step\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:160: trailing whitespace.\n+  against an unverified or wrong target, acting on detection results that have\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:161: trailing whitespace.\n+  gone stale, or repeating a failing operation in a loop instead of surfacing the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:162: trailing whitespace.\n+  real error. These are all part of correctness.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:163: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Don't** spend the complexity budget defending against adversarial input,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:164: trailing whitespace.\n+  impossible states, or bizarre configurations that won't arise in normal use.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:165: trailing whitespace.\n+  Robust where reality demands it; not paranoid where it doesn't.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:166: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:167: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:168: trailing whitespace.\n+### 8. No paternalistic or adversarial guarding — but do catch the agent's mistakes\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:169: trailing whitespace.\n+The server protects **correctness**. It does **not** protect the engineer from\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:170: trailing whitespace.\n+their own legitimate project, and it does **not** harden trusted input against\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:171: trailing whitespace.\n+attacks the deployment model says cannot happen. Over-defensive guarding is a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:172: trailing whitespace.\n+defect: it burns the complexity budget and, worse, it blocks real work.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:173: trailing whitespace.\n+Paternalism is refusing or second-guessing an operation the agent *intended* and\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:174: trailing whitespace.\n+*correctly aimed*. It is **not** paternalism to catch an operation the agent did\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:175: trailing whitespace.\n+*not* intend — one that contradicts verified state, targets the wrong or an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:176: trailing whitespace.\n+unverified device, or is the product of a thrash loop. Guarding against the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:177: trailing whitespace.\n+fallible agent's own mistakes is a correctness duty, not nannying.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:178: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:179: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:180: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Safety of the project is the engineer's job; catching mistakes is the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:181: trailing whitespace.\n+  server's.** If the engineer is *intentionally* doing something risky — erasing a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:182: trailing whitespace.\n+  bootloader, writing option bytes, flashing a part in a way that could brick it —\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:183: trailing whitespace.\n+  the server performs the requested operation (with honest verification and honest\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:184: trailing whitespace.\n+  reporting of what happened) and lets the engineer own the risk. It does not\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:185: trailing whitespace.\n+  refuse, second-guess, gate a coherent correctly-targeted request behind \"are you\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:186: trailing whitespace.\n+  sure,\" or nanny. **But an intended risky operation and a mistaken destructive\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:187: trailing whitespace.\n+  one look different to the server, and it must tell them apart by checking\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:188: trailing whitespace.\n+  against what it has verified.** Before a destructive or hard-to-reverse step,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:189: trailing whitespace.\n+  confirm the operation is consistent with verified state — the target the agent\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:190: trailing whitespace.\n+  named is the one actually on the bus, the ELF matches the detected part, the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:191: trailing whitespace.\n+  port is the one that was detected. If the request contradicts verified state,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:192: trailing whitespace.\n+  that is a mistake, not a brave choice: stop and report the mismatch instead of\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:193: trailing whitespace.\n+  faithfully executing it. The server never blocks the risk the engineer *chose*;\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:194: trailing whitespace.\n+  it does block the damage the agent *didn't mean to do*.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:195: trailing whitespace.\n+- **No adversarial-input hardening on trusted inputs.** ZIP-bomb guards,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:196: trailing whitespace.\n+  path-traversal rejection, XML-entity-expansion limits, member-count ceilings,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:197: trailing whitespace.\n+  compression-ratio checks, sandbox walls — none of these belong on a pack or\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:198: trailing whitespace.\n+  file that a compliant agent selected. They defend against an attacker this\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:199: trailing whitespace.\n+  deployment does not have, and they routinely break legitimate work (e.g. an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:200: trailing whitespace.\n+  official STM32U5 pack that expands past an arbitrary size cap).\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:201: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Every limit must trace to a real constraint.** A cap, buffer size, timeout,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:202: trailing whitespace.\n+  or count limit is allowed only when it comes from actual hardware, a protocol, a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:203: trailing whitespace.\n+  correctness requirement, or the need to stop a fallible agent from thrashing (a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:204: trailing whitespace.\n+  bounded retry/loop that ends in an honest error, so a mistake surfaces instead\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:205: trailing whitespace.\n+  of spinning) — never from a hypothetical adversary or \"just in case.\" A limit\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:206: trailing whitespace.\n+  set by the target's real flash size is correctness; a retry bound that ends in a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:207: trailing whitespace.\n+  truthful failure report is correctness. An arbitrary magic number — a 128 MiB\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:208: trailing whitespace.\n+  expanded-size ceiling with no hardware basis — is still an anti-pattern; raise\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:209: trailing whitespace.\n+  it out of the way or delete it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:210: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Keep checks that catch the tool *or the agent* getting it wrong.** Rejecting a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:211: trailing whitespace.\n+  genuinely corrupt or wrong file so the tool doesn't silently do the wrong thing\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:212: trailing whitespace.\n+  is correctness, and it stays. Catching a requested operation that contradicts\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:213: trailing whitespace.\n+  verified state — wrong target, mismatched ELF, stale detection — is correctness,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:214: trailing whitespace.\n+  and it stays. Rejecting a valid file because it *could* have been malicious is\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:215: trailing whitespace.\n+  paternalism, and it goes. Cheap malformed-file hygiene is fine when its purpose\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:216: trailing whitespace.\n+  is \"don't misprocess a broken file\" or \"don't execute an obvious mistake,\" not\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:217: trailing whitespace.\n+  \"assume the operator is hostile.\"\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:218: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:219: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:220: trailing whitespace.\n+**The test for any guard:** *\"Does removing this let a compliant but fallible\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:221: trailing whitespace.\n+agent — one that may thrash, misread state, target the wrong device, or act on an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:222: trailing whitespace.\n+unverified assumption — or a cooperative user get a wrong, destructive, or\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:223: trailing whitespace.\n+misreported outcome they did not intend?\"* If yes, it is correctness — keep it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:224: trailing whitespace.\n+If it only ever stops an attacker who isn't in this threat model, or stops the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:225: trailing whitespace.\n+user from doing something *risky-but-intended and correctly-targeted*, delete it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:226: trailing whitespace.\n+The dividing line is intent versus mistake: guard the mistake, permit the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:227: trailing whitespace.\n+intended risk.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:228: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:229: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:230: trailing whitespace.\n+#### Worked example — `flash_target` with a mismatched ELF\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:231: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:232: trailing whitespace.\n+The same scenario resolved three ways. An agent calls\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:233: trailing whitespace.\n+`flash_target(port=\"/dev/ttyACM0\", elf_path=\"build/app.elf\")`. The server has\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:234: trailing whitespace.\n+already detected, via a chip-ID read on that port, that the connected part is an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:235: trailing whitespace.\n+**STM32L4**. The ELF's headers say it was built for an **STM32F4**. Flashing an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:236: trailing whitespace.\n+F4 image to an L4 will not run and may leave the part in a state the agent then\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:237: trailing whitespace.\n+misdiagnoses.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:238: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:239: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Naive / stripped bare (wrong).** The tool flashes whatever bytes it was\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:240: trailing whitespace.\n+  handed and reports success because the write-and-verify against its own image\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:241: trailing whitespace.\n+  passed. The agent, being only OK at its job, now believes it has working\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:242: trailing whitespace.\n+  firmware on the L4 and burns the next hour debugging a \"runtime\" problem that is\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:243: trailing whitespace.\n+  really a build-target mistake. This is a **correctness failure**: the server\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:244: trailing whitespace.\n+  faithfully executed a mistake and misled the reader about what happened.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:245: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:246: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Paternalistic (also wrong).** The tool refuses to flash any image whose\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:247: trailing whitespace.\n+  target it doesn't have an allow-listed profile for, or pops \"this could brick\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:248: trailing whitespace.\n+  your board — are you sure? [y/N]\" on every flash. This blocks the engineer who\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:249: trailing whitespace.\n+  *deliberately* flashes an unusual or bring-up image to a correctly-identified\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:250: trailing whitespace.\n+  part, and it defends against nothing real. Delete it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:251: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:252: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Mistake-guard (correct).** Before writing, the tool compares the ELF's target\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:253: trailing whitespace.\n+  against the **verified** chip ID it already read from that port. They disagree,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:254: trailing whitespace.\n+  so it stops and returns an honest, actionable error: detected `STM32L4xx` on\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:255: trailing whitespace.\n+  `/dev/ttyACM0`, but `build/app.elf` targets `STM32F4xx` — refusing to flash a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:256: trailing whitespace.\n+  mismatched image; rebuild for the L4 or select the correct port/ELF. It does\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:257: trailing whitespace.\n+  **not** ask \"are you sure\"; it reports a contradiction with reality. Note the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:258: trailing whitespace.\n+  precise boundary: if chip-ID detection was **unavailable** (no readback on this\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:259: trailing whitespace.\n+  interface), the tool cannot assert a mismatch — so it does not invent one. It\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:260: trailing whitespace.\n+  flashes, states in its result that the target could not be verified against the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:261: trailing whitespace.\n+  device, and lets the engineer own that gap. The guard fires only on a *verified*\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:262: trailing whitespace.\n+  contradiction, never on an *unverified* suspicion.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:263: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:264: trailing whitespace.\n+The test applied: removing the mismatch check lets a compliant-but-fallible agent\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:265: trailing whitespace.\n+brick or mis-flash a board and be told it succeeded → correctness → keep it.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:266: trailing whitespace.\n+Removing the \"are you sure\" prompt costs a compliant agent nothing except a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:267: trailing whitespace.\n+speed bump on work it meant to do → paternalism → delete it. Same tool, opposite\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:268: trailing whitespace.\n+verdicts, decided entirely by *verified state* and *intent versus mistake*.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:269: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:270: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:271: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:272: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:273: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:274: trailing whitespace.\n+## When principles collide\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:275: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Simplicity vs Generalizability** → choose the simplest design that still\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:276: trailing whitespace.\n+  handles the unknown *by detection*, not the most abstract one. Runtime\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:277: trailing whitespace.\n+  detection beats an abstraction layer.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:278: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Generalizability / Dynamism vs Correctness** → generality never licenses\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:279: trailing whitespace.\n+  guessing. On novel hardware, **verify** (read back, confirm IDs) instead of\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:280: trailing whitespace.\n+  assuming; if it can't be verified, report the uncertainty and let the user decide how to proceed.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:281: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Correctness vs Simplicity** → correctness wins — but once the real\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:282: trailing whitespace.\n+  requirement is understood, the correct implementation is usually also the\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:283: trailing whitespace.\n+  simpler one.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:284: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Normal-case assumption vs Correctness** → assuming the normal case decides\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:285: trailing whitespace.\n+  *which situations to handle*. It is never an excuse for a tool to misreport\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:286: trailing whitespace.\n+  what happened.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:287: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Defensive guarding vs Functionality** → within the trust boundary,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:288: trailing whitespace.\n+  functionality wins. A guard that blocks legitimate work to defend against an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:289: trailing whitespace.\n+  actor this deployment model excludes — or to protect the engineer from an\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:290: trailing whitespace.\n+  intended, correctly-targeted risky operation — is removed, not tuned. But a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:291: trailing whitespace.\n+  guard that catches the fallible agent's own mistake — an operation contradicting\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:292: trailing whitespace.\n+  verified state, a destructive step on an unverified target, an unbounded thrash\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:293: trailing whitespace.\n+  loop — is a **correctness** guard, not defensive guarding, and it stays.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:294: trailing whitespace.\n+  Correctness guards (verify, don't fabricate, don't misreport, don't misprocess a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:295: trailing whitespace.\n+  broken file, don't faithfully execute a mistake) are non-negotiable.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:296: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:297: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:298: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:299: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:300: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:301: trailing whitespace.\n+## Definition of done\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:302: trailing whitespace.\n+A change is done when all of these hold:\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:303: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:304: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:305: trailing whitespace.\n+1. It does the intended thing and reports honestly (no silent failure, no\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:306: trailing whitespace.\n+   fabricated results).\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:307: trailing whitespace.\n+2. It is the simplest form that is still effective — nothing speculative.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:308: trailing whitespace.\n+3. It contains no environment-specific constants; it detects or parameterizes\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:309: trailing whitespace.\n+   everything that varies.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:310: trailing whitespace.\n+4. It is cleanly named and lives in the one place that owns its responsibility.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:311: trailing whitespace.\n+5. Its tool docs let an agent use it correctly with zero outside knowledge,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:312: trailing whitespace.\n+   including how to recover from its common failures.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:313: trailing whitespace.\n+6. A novel-but-reasonable input is handled by composing existing tools, not by\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:314: trailing whitespace.\n+   editing source.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:315: trailing whitespace.\n+7. Every guard, limit, and rejection it introduces traces to a correctness or\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:316: trailing whitespace.\n+   real hardware/protocol constraint — none of it hardens against a hostile actor\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:317: trailing whitespace.\n+   outside the threat model, and none of it nannies the engineer out of a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:318: trailing whitespace.\n+   legitimate, correctly-targeted operation they chose to run. Guards that catch\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:319: trailing whitespace.\n+   the fallible agent's own mistakes — a request contradicting verified state, a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:320: trailing whitespace.\n+   destructive step on an unverified target, an unbounded retry loop — are\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:321: trailing whitespace.\n+   correctness guards and are expected to be present, not stripped.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:322: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:323: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:324: trailing whitespace.\n+---\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:325: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:326: trailing whitespace.\n+\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:327: trailing whitespace.\n+## Anti-patterns — reject these\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:328: trailing whitespace.\n+- Hardcoded ports/paths, OS checks that break elsewhere, fixed core or toolchain\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:329: trailing whitespace.\n+  assumptions.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:330: trailing whitespace.\n+- Silent failure, swallowed errors, or fabricated success/data.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:331: trailing whitespace.\n+- Speculative abstraction, unused config knobs, a plugin framework with one\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:332: trailing whitespace.\n+  plugin.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:333: trailing whitespace.\n+- Detection or config logic scattered across many tools; inconsistent parameter\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:334: trailing whitespace.\n+  names or units.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:335: trailing whitespace.\n+- Tools with missing docs, or docs that omit failure modes and recovery.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:336: trailing whitespace.\n+- A `switch`/`if` ladder that must grow every time a new chip appears — that is a\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:337: trailing whitespace.\n+  detection-plus-generic-path job instead.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:338: trailing whitespace.\n+- Any hardcoded systems or boards or part numbers, or any features that are designed to target only a specific few listed systems and bias towards those systems without offering the same improvement to all other possible systems in existence.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:339: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Adversarial-input hardening on trusted input** — ZIP-bomb / entity-expansion\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:340: trailing whitespace.\n+  / path-traversal / member-count / compression-ratio guards applied to a pack\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:341: trailing whitespace.\n+  or file the compliant agent chose. That defends against an actor outside this\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:342: trailing whitespace.\n+  threat model.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:343: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Paternalistic refusals, prompts, or caps** that block a legitimate,\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:344: trailing whitespace.\n+  correctly-targeted, *intended* risky operation on the engineer's own hardware.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:345: trailing whitespace.\n+  The engineer owns the risk of their own project. (This is **not** a license to\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:346: trailing whitespace.\n+  drop guards that catch the agent's *mistakes* — an operation contradicting\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:347: trailing whitespace.\n+  verified state, a destructive step on an unverified target, or a thrash loop.\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:348: trailing whitespace.\n+  Those are correctness guards and must stay.)\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:349: trailing whitespace.\n+- **Arbitrary magic-number limits** (size / buffer / count / timeout) not derived\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:350: trailing whitespace.\n+  from a real hardware, protocol, or correctness constraint — e.g. a fixed\r\n.change-loop/design_charter.md:351: trailing whitespace.\n+  expanded-size ceiling that blocks an official current vendor pack.\r\n.change-loop/loop-driver.stderr.log:19: trailing whitespace.\n+EEEEEEEEEEEFEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE.EEEEFEEEEEFEEEEEE\r\n.change-loop/loop-driver.stderr.log:20: trailing whitespace.\n+======================================================================\r\n.change-loop/loop-driver.stderr.log:21: trailing whitespace.\n+ERROR: test_breakpoint_tools (unittest.loader._FailedTest.test_breakpoint_tools)\r\n.change-loop/loop-driver.stderr.log:22: trailing whitespace.\n+---------------------------------------------